{
  "validity": "pass",
  "quality": "adequate",
  "issues": [
    {
      "kind": "annotation",
      "location": "obligations[0].answer_attributes",
      "problem": "The prompt only asks for the message text ('pull up the text of that message'), but answer_attributes includes both messages.message_text and messages.channel_id. The channel_id is a supplemental identifying/answer field that was never requested in the prompt, violating the rule that supplemental identifying conditions must be requested, not secretly enforced.",
      "repair": "Restrict answer_attributes to [[\"messages.message_text\"]] for both candidate role sets, dropping messages.channel_id unless the prompt is revised to ask for channel context as well."
    }
  ],
  "rationale": "The design correctly builds a genuine underspecified case: two distinct workspace members named 'Robert' (Robert Walsh in #engineering, Robert Chen in #core-infra) each tada-react to a different message, with no delegated tie-breaking authority in the prompt, matching the underspecified resolution mode. The route (MESSAGE→REACTION→USER→CONVERSATION_MEMBERSHIP→CONVERSATION→WORKSPACE) is respected without collapsing into an unjustified shortcut, and the bot's channel memberships (C03IJKL9012, C_INFRA per channel_members) support the described access plan via conversations.history, reactions.get, and users.info — all real, documented APIs, with no reliance on unavailable workspace-name lookups or hidden timestamp fields. The two negatives (same reactor wrong emoji; same emoji wrong reactor) are well-motivated and don't leak the answer. The planned new messages/reactions not yet present in the base seed are acceptable at this design stage per the given allowance for compiler-created rows. The one substantive flaw is that the answer_attributes obligation smuggles in messages.channel_id, an attribute never requested by the prompt, which is an annotation-level defect rather than a structural validity failure, so the design passes with a required repair before compilation is treated as final."
}

{
  "validity": "pass",
  "quality": "strong",
  "issues": [],
  "rationale": "This revision resolves the prior defect: answer_attributes is now limited to messages.message_text, matching exactly what the prompt requests ('pull up the text of that message'), with no unrequested supplemental field smuggled into the obligation. The core design remains sound: two workspace members named 'Robert' (Robert Walsh in #engineering, Robert Chen in #core-infra per channel_members) each tada-react to a distinct message, creating a genuine underspecified referent with no delegated tie-breaking authority in the prompt — correctly modeled via candidate_role_sets and selection 'one'. The route (MESSAGE→REACTION→USER→CONVERSATION_MEMBERSHIP→CONVERSATION→WORKSPACE) is exercised without shortcuts, and the access plan uses only documented, realistic calls (conversations.history on channels the bot belongs to per channel_members, reactions.get, users.info for display-name confirmation) without relying on unavailable workspace-name lookups or hidden timestamp fields. The negatives are now each paired with an explicit supporting reaction record (walsh_thumbsup_reaction, lukas_tada_reaction), cleanly isolating the two dimensions of exclusion (wrong emoji from the matching reactor; correct emoji from a non-matching reactor) without leaking the answer. Planned new messages/reactions not yet in the base seed are acceptable at this design stage. The prompt is short, natural, and the two-Robert ambiguity gives the case a meaningful, non-trivial evaluation target."
}

{
  "validity": "pass",
  "quality": "strong",
  "issues": [],
  "rationale": "The compiled artifacts faithfully realize the reviewed design. Seed edits insert exactly the four planned messages and four reactions (Robert Walsh→login-fix tada, Robert Chen→Tokyo-latency tada, Robert Walsh→coffee-machine thumbsup negative, Lukas→quantization tada negative), matching the roles' facts and the base_seed's existing channel memberships (U03ROBERT23 in C03IJKL9012 and C02EFGH5678; U_ROBERT in C_INFRA), so the negatives are genuinely reachable by the acting bot (U01AGENBOT9, a member of C03IJKL9012, C_INFRA, C02EFGH5678, and C_MODEL) via conversations.history/reactions.get/users.info as claimed in the access plan. The focal selector traverses the full assigned route (messages→message_reactions→users→channel_members→channels→teams) rather than shortcutting through the more direct user_teams table, honoring route R090's requirement to pass through CONVERSATION_MEMBERSHIP/CONVERSATION en route to WORKSPACE. The team_name filter is acknowledged as non-restrictive (single-team seed) rather than hidden or load-bearing, which is consistent with the exposure_qualification's visible-ID/bounded-corpus constraint. Mechanical checks report zero errors, and computed_matches ([1706120000.000001, 1706120100.000002]) exactly match the two candidate_role_sets from the design, while computed_focal_negatives correctly place the thumbsup and non-Robert-tada messages outside the match set. Answer-computation attributes are limited to messages.message_text, matching the prompt's sole request for message text, with no unrequested supplemental field. The underspecified resolution mode is preserved end-to-end (candidate_sets present, selection 'one', require_near_miss true), and no calibration or scope exception was inappropriately introduced. This is a clean, well-grounded compilation of an already-approved design with no new defects."
}

{
  "validity": "pass",
  "quality": "strong",
  "issues": [],
  "rationale": "The prompt asks for the message reacted to by 'someone named Robert who's a member of #general', which correctly translates to a reactor-side channel-membership condition rather than requiring the message itself to reside in #general — the design's binding_note makes this explicit ('neither message needs to be in #general itself, only the reactor's membership does'), and the compiled selector implements exactly that: it joins message_reactions.user_id → channel_members.user_id → channels filtered on channel_name='general', independent of the message's own channel_id. This is a legitimate, prompt-faithful use of the CONVERSATION_MEMBERSHIP/CONVERSATION route nodes, correctly distinguishing 'reactor's channel membership' from 'message's channel' (the exact distinction the review guidance warns about), and it is now genuinely restrictive (unlike a prior iteration's non-restrictive team_name-only join) because channel_name='general' is a real filter that excludes Robert Kim.\n\nThe underspecified ambiguity is properly preserved: two #general-member Roberts (Walsh via preexisting membership, Chen via a declared seed edit) each tada-react to a distinct message, yielding candidate_sets of size one each and computed_matches exactly matching those two message IDs. The negative structure is richer and more targeted than earlier iterations, isolating three independent failure dimensions with dedicated seed_edits and reaction rows: wrong emoji from a qualifying Robert (Walsh/thumbsup), right emoji from a non-Robert (Lukas), and right name+right emoji from a Robert who fails the specific #general-membership condition (Kim, added as a new user with only #random membership) — this last negative directly and effectively exercises the CONVERSATION_MEMBERSHIP node's real semantic role. Mechanical checks report zero errors, and computed_focal_negatives/candidate_population confirm no accidental extra matches (e.g., preexisting Robert-authored messages like 1699824000.000987 and 1706067472 correctly remain unreacted-to and excluded, preserving the authorship-vs-reaction distinction).\n\nAccess is realistic: the acting bot (U01AGENBOT9) already holds memberships in #general, #engineering, #core-infra, #random, and #model-research, covering every channel needed to discover the two matches and all three negatives via conversations.members/conversations.history/reactions.get/users.info, with no reliance on workspace-name enumeration beyond the visible, non-restrictive team_name predicate. Answer-computation attributes are limited to messages.message_text, matching exactly what the prompt requests with no supplemental unrequested field. The task is a clean, well-motivated read-only retrieval with a meaningful three-way negative structure and no defects found."
}
